  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a direct relationship between Equine Botulinum Neurotoxin C Immune FAB2 and Prostaglandin E2 receptor EP4 subtype, mediated through CDK3/Cyclin E. This pathway is supported by multiple high-scoring connections, with each intermediate entity—Kutzneride 9, (2R)-2-[(1R)-...]-hydroxyethyl-...-6-one, and 2-(5-Hydroxy-1H-Indol-3-Yl)Acetic Acid—acting as a target for the toxin. These targets are all known to interact with the EP4 receptor, indicating a potential mechanism by which the toxin influences receptor activity. The consistency across different pathways strengthens the hypothesis that the toxin's interaction with CDK3/Cyclin E leads to a downstream effect on the EP4 receptor, possibly through a signaling cascade. The evidence highlights a recurring role of CDK3/Cyclin E as a central node in this relationship, underscoring its importance in linking the toxin to the receptor.",
